= Discussion <sec:disc>

This chapter reads the results of Chapter 6 together. It first states what the evidence supports, rung by rung, and how far it answers the research questions of Chapter 1 (@sec:disc-supports). It then looks at where the model's proposals stop on their way to the robots: in grounding refusals, and in how little of the model's plan reaches execution (@sec:disc-grounding). The last two sections set out the limitations (@sec:disc-limits) and the threats to validity, including those that arose after the design was fixed (@sec:disc-threats). Throughout, the four grades of evidence of Chapter 6 stay apart, and nothing is pooled across experiments, across the two IB10 runs, or with the demonstration takes.

== What the Evidence Supports <sec:disc-supports>

The evidence register orders the claims of this report as a ladder of six rungs, from what the evidence supports most directly to what it does not support at all #ev("6.16"). Each rung below is stated with the sections of Chapter 6 that carry it.

*a. The pipeline clears the intersection jam (@sec:res-ib10-ab, @sec:res-ib10-c).* A Layer 2 trigger plus M3 grounding and phased dispatch cleared the four-robot intersection jam in 4 of 5 episodes, against 0 of 5 with Layer 2 off, where the robots did not move again once the jam had formed #ev("6.05") #ev("6.30"). In the separate second run, the live-VLM arm cleared it in 5 of 5 (4 of 4 without the smoke episode, whose acceptance deviated from the registered procedure) and the scripted arm that skipped the VLM call and ran M3's default plan in 4 of 4 valid episodes (5 of 5 after a post hoc re-run), in similar time; every first dispatch of the live-VLM arm re-grounds to exactly the scripted arm's plan #ev("6.06"). Both runs are exploratory pilots, five episodes per arm on one near-deterministic scene, and they are never pooled. The rung is a claim about the pipeline. The default plan clears this jam without the model's plan, and the model's remaining role in this scene is to choose the strategy and the robot set, which the scripted arm was given.

*b. The model returns valid decisions (@sec:res-demo, @sec:res-exploratory).* ER-2 returned a valid, parseable decision in all 67 analyses of the demonstration campaign #ev("6.44"). Valid does not mean usable: in two intersection takes the answers parsed but their plans were nulled for invalid structure #ev("6.39"). Nor is validity model-general. Sonnet 5.5 refused 30 of 48 real-frame runs under the original prompt ending and none under a JSON-only ending #ev("6.09") #ev("6.11"), and gpt-4o's refusals in an earlier replay were triggered by the real camera frame #ev("4.37").

*c. The gate, not the model, secures a feasible yielder in the two-robot scenes (@sec:res-headon).* In the recorded head-on decisions the model's yielder never beat the better of two trivial baselines, and with the gate on the grounded yielder was correct in 79 of 79 runs, after the gate had overturned 37 model picks #ev("6.08"). The demonstration head-on take shows the same mechanism once: the model named a robot without room to back out, and the gate handed the yield to the other #ev("6.37").

*d. A plan can be accepted and executed, rarely, and with small effect (@sec:res-plan, @sec:res-demo, @sec:res-demo-ib).* In the live four-robot analyses before the campaign, the model's plan changed the dispatched commands in 0 of the 39 analyses that pass the trace's fidelity gate #ev("6.02"). In the campaign, the verifier accepted the plan unchanged in 6 of 65 plan-channel analyses as recorded, and 5 of these were dispatched as written by the offline recomputation #ev("6.44") #ev("6.51"). A plan changed the executed commands once, in intersection take 5, by setting the order of two back-out manoeuvres whose costs differ by 0.010 m against a tolerance of 0.15 m; only the timing of the two back-outs changed #ev("6.39"). The take was chosen for its label, and the prompt listed the plan actions the verifier accepts, so the model chose among offered options #ev("6.03").

*e. What the evidence does not support (@sec:res-ib10-c, @sec:res-headon, @sec:res-demo-convoy, @sec:res-demo-five, @sec:res-t1).* The evidence does not show that the model's decision improves outcomes. The live-VLM and scripted arms of the second IB10 run are indistinguishable, and of the episodes in which the model's own decision fields changed execution, neither of the two that can be judged resolved #ev("6.06") #ev("6.02"). It does not show that the model uses the scene: no tested model met the pre-registered scene-use rule in the offline replays #ev("6.09") #ev("6.11"), and the one lead from the improved-annotation replay is not an assessable result #ev("6.10"). And it does not show that the pipeline handles congestion outside the intersection: all five convoy takes ended unresolved #ev("6.40") #ev("6.41"), the five-robot take ended with one of five robots arrived #ev("6.42"), and in the registered tranche-1 pilot no episode of either arm completed its task stream, with a throughput interval that includes zero #ev("6.14").

*f. Safety evidence is narrow (@sec:res-safety).* The safety evidence is the ground-truth centre distance between robot pairs. The simulator's contact lists were hard-coded empty in the evaluated runtime #ev("6.20"), and one sustained robot-obstacle contact is confirmed, in the five-robot take, where an escort drove `robot_3` into rack island `obstacle_2` #ev("6.42"). Passing the robot-pair gates is not a general safety claim.

=== Answers to the Research Questions <sec:disc-rq>

The answers follow the map of @tab:intro-rq-map and are no broader than the rungs above.

- *RQ1, perception and classification,* is answered in part and at pilot scale. In tranche 1, Arm A detected 9 of 11 ground-truth conflicts after the stall trigger, with no false positive in 26 positive analyses, a type accuracy of 0.778 and an involved-set F1 of 0.915 #ev("6.35") #ev("6.14"). These are five seeds of one scene.
- *RQ2, strategy competence,* is answered for the pipeline, not for the model. The pipeline clears the intersection jam that Layer 1 leaves in place (rung a). The model's share of that clearing is bounded by the scripted arm and the plan-influence trace, and the tranche-1 pilot has no completed episode in either arm (rungs d and e).
- *RQ3, social reasoning,* is informed only at the decision level. The grounded yielder was made correct by the gate, the model's choice followed robot labels or listing order rather than the scene where this could be tested, and the one back-out order set by the model that was executed was a near-tie (rungs c, d and e).
- *RQ4, semantic advantage,* and *RQ5, closed-loop adaptation,* are not tested by any experiment in this report.

== Where Proposals Stop: Grounding Refusals and Plan Use <sec:disc-grounding>

Once a jam has been detected, a model's proposal can stop in two places before it reaches the robots. Grounding can refuse it, or grounding can execute a command that owes nothing to the proposed plan. Chapter 6 shows both often. This section collects the evidence for each and states what it does not yet show.

*Refusals.* In the five-robot S1b scene, re-grounding the recorded live analyses showed that 78 analyses gave 13 motion commands. Deterministic M3 refusals withheld 47 of the 78 analyses, and one refusal class, the static gate of `junction_clear`, accounted for 27 of the 47. The model is not told that a decision was withheld, and 32 of the 47 re-asked a scene that had already been refused #ev("6.15"). The registered tranche shows the same pattern seed by seed: of Arm A's analyses, 4, 2, 3, 4 and 1 were withheld per seed, against 5, 2, 2, 1 and 2 that reached M4 #ev("6.14"). In the Stage 1 shadow the ordinary loop dispatched 14 analyses, withheld 33 and had no decision in 3 #ev("6.47"). The demonstration takes show the pattern once more, as illustrations. In the five-robot take, six of eight decisions were withheld by grounding refusals #ev("6.42"). In the first three convoy takes every later proposal ran as an 8 s wait, because grounding refused the yields and reverses and the other strategies have no template in the legacy grounding used there; offline, 72 of 76 hypothetical decisions on post-latch snapshots also became waits #ev("6.40"). The offline bench points the same way from the other side: the per-cluster format raised cluster coverage, but a chooser that takes the first listed option did as well, so the gain belongs to the format and to the grounder's own option order, not to the model's choice #ev("6.46").

*Plan use.* Where grounding does dispatch, the model's plan rarely shapes the command. In the evaluated runtime the grounder never reads the proposed plan when it grounds; it grounds from the decision fields and audits the plan afterwards, and the plan changed the dispatched commands in 0 of 39 gated analyses #ev("6.02"). In the campaign, where an opt-in switch let a verified plan set the back-out order at the intersection, the plan changed the executed commands once, and only in timing #ev("6.39"). Through the decision fields, the model's other choices did change execution, in 18 dispatches in four episodes, and where the effect can be judged neither episode resolved #ev("6.49") #ev("6.02").

*Plan versus dispatch.* The provenance label itself over-states how far plan and dispatch agree. For `reverse` and `reroute` the grounder acts on the model's list of involved robots and never reads the plan, and the verifier puts no constraint on a `reverse` plan, so a plan can be recorded as accepted unchanged while the dispatch contradicts it #ev("6.51") #ev("6.80"). Over the recorded live decisions, 10 of 33 dispatched `reverse` legs went to robots whose plan said something else, and 3 of 3 dispatched reroutes rerouted a different robot from the one the plan named #ev("6.51") #ev("6.80"). Outside `junction_clear`, of 15 decisions labelled `vlm_verbatim` whose dispatch differed from the plan, 9 are true overrides (5 `reverse`, 3 `reroute` and one convoy yield) and 6 are a registered artefact of the retreat shield #ev("6.80"). These are decision-level counts over recorded decisions, not a comparison between series. With the corrected definition the campaign's 6 accepted plans become 5, and tranche 1's 2 become 1; both are recomputations beside the recorded labels, which stay unchanged #ev("6.51"). A fix that makes the mismatch visible in the decision record and the logs was built behind a key that is off by default; it changes neither the prompt nor the grounding and has not run live #ev("6.51"). One consequence remains open. The plan state that M5 shows back to the model in later prompts is still wrong in these cases, for example "wait (done)" for a robot that in fact backed out. Correcting it would change the model's input, which was ruled out at the time of writing #ev("6.80").

*Execution paths.* Not every stop is a refusal. In the five-robot take the "unresolved" label partly reflects an escort path that drove `robot_3` into a rack island, a path that no stage checked against static obstacles #ev("6.42") #ev("6.43"). In convoy takes 4 and 5 a grounding change held the queued robots as built, but the model again named the yielder that the scene is designed to defeat, and the escort latched against a held robot #ev("6.41").

*What this does not show.* Taken together, these results show where many proposals stopped. They do not yet show which stage caused the unfinished episodes: a refused proposal may have been unsound, a dispatched one may have failed in execution, and the scene itself may have left no way out. The failure-cause classification of @sec:res-failure-causes asks this question, by stage, for the five-robot episodes, and it may also leave a large share of units undetermined. #todo[TBD-D01: one or two sentences on what the failure-cause classification shows, per stratum and without pooling, once the reconciliation report exists; until then this section makes no claim about which stage limits the pipeline.]

== Limitations <sec:disc-limits>

The limitations below bound every claim of this report. Each names the evidence behind it.

- *The model does not use the scene for the yielder choice.* No tested model met the pre-registered scene-use rule, gpt-4o followed the listing slot in 85 of 96 grey-pair yields, and the gate, not the model, made the grounded yielder correct #ev("6.09") #ev("6.08"). The improved-annotation replay that could change this for ER-2 was stopped by provider limits #ev("6.10").
- *Plans have almost no executed effect.* The plan changed dispatch in 0 of 39 gated live four-robot analyses, and once in the campaign, in back-out timing only #ev("6.02") #ev("6.39").
- *Constrained selection.* The campaign's intersection prompt listed the actions the verifier accepts, so take 5 is a choice among offered options, not free planning #ev("6.03").
- *Provenance labels over-state agreement.* For `reverse` and `reroute` a plan recorded as accepted unchanged can coexist with a dispatch that contradicts it (@sec:disc-grounding). The corrected counts are an offline recomputation, the visibility fix has not run live, and the plan state shown back to the model is still wrong after such overrides #ev("6.51") #ev("6.80").
- *Valid does not mean usable.* Every campaign answer parsed, but some plans were nulled for invalid structure #ev("6.39").
- *Grounding refuses most proposals in the five-robot scene,* and the model is not told that a decision was withheld #ev("6.15").
- *No recovery for latched clusters.* In the convoy scene no legacy strategy grounds to motion after the latch #ev("6.40"). The queued-robot hold built for it was used in two takes, which stayed unresolved; whether a hold of this kind can resolve the scene is not shown #ev("6.19") #ev("6.41").
- *Static obstacles were not checked along escort segments or reverse legs.* In the evaluated runtime only the yielder's retreat room and retreat candidates used the declared boxes, and neither M4's heading primitive nor its safety monitor looks at obstacles #ev("6.43"). A validation take came within 5.5 mm of a declared box under a `reverse` #ev("6.17"), and earlier `reverse` legs had pinned robots against box faces #ev("6.18"). The guards built afterwards are off by default and see declared obstacles only. The escort guard ran live once, armed, and never fired, because no escort was grounded #ev("6.17"). The reverse guard rests on unit tests and an offline replay and has not run live #ev("6.13"). The stall abort, which would end a primitive whose commanded motion produces no movement, has unit and node tests and an offline replay, and no live run #ev("6.18").
- *Contact measurement is partial.* The ground-truth contact lists were a stub in every evaluated cell #ev("6.20"). The geometric logger is only partially validated: its fields were written on every row of one validation take and reproduced offline, but no robot was pinned during it, so the take does not show entries appearing when one is #ev("6.17"). The offline recompute over recorded bundles stays pending validation #ev("6.12").
- *The safety-exemption fix is not validated live.* It was on in IB10 run 2, where it never fired, and in the demonstration takes, where its firings were not counted; neither is a live validation #ev("6.07") #ev("6.06") #ev("5.51").
- *Small samples and near-repeat episodes.* Five episodes or seed pairs per arm, one scene per live experiment, one to five takes per demonstration scene, and small offline replays #ev("6.05") #ev("6.06") #ev("6.14") #ev("6.45").
- *One VLM in the live comparisons.* ER-2 ran every live comparison and every campaign take; the other models ran in earlier pilots or offline only #ev("6.16").
- *Ascending listing order in the live cells.* The yielder and back-out-order choices of the live cells cannot separate an identifier prior from a slot prior (@sec:res-exploratory) #ev("6.39").
- *Configuration drift.* The campaign ran a later prompt and, at the intersection, the plan-order switch, so it is not the evaluated runtime #ev("6.39").
- *No classical comparator, and two questions untested.* Every live comparison measures pipeline on against pipeline off, or against the grounder's default; RQ4 and RQ5 are not tested (@sec:intro-rq).

=== Failure-Cause Classification <sec:disc-failure-cause>

The classification of @sec:res-failure-causes rests on a rule that its authors wrote after reading earlier diagnoses of some of the episodes, to be applied by two raters to 33 episodes in three strata that are never pooled #ev("6.60") #ev("6.61"). It is exploratory and post hoc, its counts are small, and the interception analysis is offline: it shows what three mechanisms would have refused or aborted, not that any scene would then have resolved. The mechanisms themselves are default-off additions that have been validated offline, and at most armed once live (@sec:res-failure-causes). Under the rule as written, the first geometry code and the code for a missing strategy cannot fire at an onset in these data, and a command that pinned a robot for longer than the 60 s link window before the onset is not linked to the unit; the sensitivity analysis S1 relaxes the second point and is reported beside the primary counts #ev("6.61"). #todo[TBD-D02: add any limitation that the reconciliation report states, such as the share left undetermined and the rater agreement, per stratum.]

== Threats to Validity <sec:disc-threats>

The threats stated in advance in @sec:ch5-threats all stand: M3 repairs or replaces the model's plan, the Layer-2-off control is a deterministic freeze, Arm C's timing differs, the S1b seeds were not unseen, the samples are small and the IB10 episodes are near-repeats, each live result comes from one scene in simulation with one model on one runtime, and the safety gates cover robot pairs only. The threats below arose after the design was fixed.

*Selection of demonstration takes.* The take rule stopped a scene at the first take that met its demonstration goal, intersection take 5 was shown because of its label, and the earlier ER2LIVE run was chosen because it qualifies #ev("5.51") #ev("6.45") #ev("6.38"). The campaign therefore illustrates what the pipeline can do. It is not a sample of what the pipeline usually does, and no rate is drawn from it.

*Analyses designed after the results.* The head-on re-score, the plan-influence trace and the plan-dispatch recomputation were defined after the live data existed #ev("6.08") #ev("6.02") #ev("6.51"). The failure-cause rule was written after the tranche-1 results and after its authors had read earlier diagnoses of some episodes; its raters were not shown those diagnoses, but the rule is not blind to them #ev("6.60") #ev("6.61").

*Reconstructed counterfactuals.* The guard replays and the stall-abort replay run recorded inputs through code written after the runs, and the stall-abort replay regenerates command streams that were not recorded #ev("6.13") #ev("6.18"). The re-groundings are trusted only where a fidelity check reproduces the recorded dispatch #ev("6.02"). None of these replays is a recording of what a live run would have done.

*Errors in the source reports.* The source reports are not free of error. The tranche-1 report's dispatched column counted a refused yield as dispatched, and this report uses the corrected counts #ev("6.14"). Other errors of this kind may remain.

*Unpushed and unreviewed sources.* The source reports cited in Chapter 6 sit on local branches that had not been pushed or reviewed when the evidence register was compiled #ev("6.16"). A review could change numbers.
